Subject: Deferred counting: a young-cell nursery for compiled plain-object construction

What this does
  - quickjs.c: plain objects built by compiled constructors are bump-allocated in 64 KB chunks with a biased reference count and an epoch stamp; a collection promotes the cells found by the root pin and drops the rest; the unit reaches the allocator through qjs_ycls/qjs_y_refill.

Why we need it
  Earleyboyer allocation and reclaim cost: dead cells never reach the zero-count table.

Soundness
  Young cells are counted only when promoted; every cell reachable from a root at a collection is promoted before anything is freed. Needs the unit prelude that stores into young cells (generator side, not yet part of the default unit).

Failure modes
  Default-off until the unit prelude ships with the generator; an engine without the prelude never allocates young cells.

